oath/oath
oathrs/target/
.claude/

# Signing keys and bearer tokens must never enter the repository. `oath keygen`
# writes <prefix>.key (private) and <prefix>.pub (public) into the working
# directory, and a tokens file is a plain-JSON list of shared secrets — both are
# one `git add -A` away from being published in an append-only, tamper-evident
# journal that also happens to be a public git history.
*.key
*.pem
tokens.json
*tokens.json
# Public keys are ignored too, which is NOT a secrecy measure — a pubkey is
# public by definition and leaks nothing. It is about deliberateness. Committing
# one binds a git identity (real name, email) to a registry principal in a
# record that is immutable, forked, and archived: unpublishable after the fact.
# It also invites the repo to be read as the trust root when the authoritative
# list of who may publish is the registry's --authorized-keys, so a committed
# copy goes silently stale at the first rotation while still looking canonical.
# Publishing a key as a deliberate trust root remains fine, and `git add -f`
# is the friction that keeps it deliberate.
*.pub
oath/oath-vector-score
__pycache__/

# git worktrees created for parallel work; never part of a commit
.worktrees/
